  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nCurrently our region is under the influence of broad south-westerly \nflow associated with a potent upper-level low that has since made \nits way inland along the Pacific Coast. As the day progresses, \nexpect further building of clouds and precipitation. Downslope winds \nare occurring across the Magic Valley and southern snake plain \nresulting in gusty south-easterly winds pushing temperatures into \nthe low 40s. Snow levels today will remain generally around 5500 ft, \nfavoring high elevation snow and valley rain. The greatest \nconcentration of impactful snow will be in the Central Mountains, \nwhere a Winter Weather Advisory remains in effect. Expect an \nadditional 3-6 inches before snow finally begins to taper off as we \nhead into the overnight period.\n\nAttention shifts to the eastern portion of our area as the low will \nbe moving through the region bringing an appreciable chance for \naccumulating snow. Confidence has increased enough to issue Winter \nWeather Advisories for much of the region. Winter Storm Warnings \nhave been issues as well for the highest elevations. In the advisory \nareas, expect 2-4 inches for valley floors and 4-8 inches for areas \nup to 6500 ft. 10-20 inches of snow is possible for the highest of \npeaks in the Bear River range and the Big Holes where the Winter \nStorm Warning is issued. There is still some uncertainty regarding \namounts, especially in the lower elevations. The National Blend of \nModels (NBM) displays a 2-3 inch range for valley levels, while the \nHRRR and RRFS are slightly more aggressive in totals, pushing the \nhigher end of the forecast range. This is likely due to the CAMs \ndepicting localized banding and NW flow orientation which may favor \nsome locally higher amounts compared to the current forecast. As of \nnow, the 2-4 inch range seems reasonable. \n\nConditions look to quiet down as we progress into mid-day on \nSaturday as the influence of an upper-level ridge will begin to move \ninto the region. Clouds will begin the clear out allowing for partly \nsunny conditions, and temperatures will remain on the cool side \ntopping out in the 30s with lows bottoming out in the teens to \nlow 20s. On Sunday, clouds will once again begin to increase as \nanother piece of energy begins to make its way toward our \nregion. The low amplitude trough will likely keep temperatures \nbelow normal, and any precipitation that does occur should be \nlight. Tuesday looks dry as another ridge looks to set in.\n\nAs we progress towards the Thanksgiving holiday, forecast variability \nincreases. Deterministic ECMWF and GFS display two different \nscenarios. ECMWF favors a soggy holiday but the GFS favors upper-\nlevel ridging and pleasant conditions. Based on current \nprobabilistic guidance, the drier solution looks to be the more \nlikely solution at this range.",
